+/// `،` `۔` `।` `。` `、` …) and opening brackets with the word after them,
+/// never breaks at a no-break space (U+00A0, U+202F) or word joiner, breaks
+/// after a hyphen or a Tibetan tsheg, and breaks at a zero-width space
+/// (U+200B) — the word marker of Thai, Lao, Khmer and Burmese copy.
+/// Tailorings on top of the default algorithm:
+/// - a dash after a space stays on the row of the word before it
+///   (`word —`, `word –`), so no row starts with a dash;
+/// - an opening quote keeps the spaces and word after it (`« mot`), and a
+///   closing quote the spaces and word before it (`mot »`), as UAX #14 15.1
+///   (LB15a/b) does — the vendored tables are 15.0;
+/// - a currency or percent sign stays with its number across spaces
+///   (`₹ 240`, `50 %`);
+/// - Korean keeps its words whole and breaks at spaces only (as with word
+///   boundaries before), also where Hangul touches Latin letters or digits;
+/// - text in a script written without spaces between words (Thai, Lao,
+///   Khmer, Burmese) that carries no zero-width space breaks between its
+///   grapheme clusters, as it did before: UAX #14 has no word dictionary,
+///   so such a run would otherwise be one unbreakable word.
